WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.012 --> 00:00:02.220
SANDEEP DINESH: When it
comes to distributed systems,

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:03.940
handling failure is key.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.960
Kubernetes helps with this
by utilizing controllers

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.880
that can watch the
state of your system

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.790
and then restart services
that stop performing.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.590
But on the other
hand, Kubernetes

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.440
can actually forcibly
terminate your applications

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.480
to ensure the overall
health of the system.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.740
In this episode of
Kubernetes Best Practices,

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:23.740
let's take a look at how
you can help Kubernetes

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.800
do its job more efficiently
and reduce the downtime

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.488
your apps experience.

00:00:28.488 --> 00:00:29.464
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:39.120
In the pre-container
world, most applications

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.990
were running on VMs
or physical machines.

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:45.380
If an application crashed,
it could take quite a while

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.990
to boot up a replacement
to take its place.

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:51.230
Or even worse, someone would
need to log into the machine

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:52.970
and fix the issue by hand--

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:54.900
even in the middle of the night.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.820
If you only had
one or two machines

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.450
to run the application,
this time to recovery

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:01.400
was unacceptable.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.970
So instead, it became common
to use process-level monitoring

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:07.760
to restart applications
when they would crash.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.370
If the application crashed,
the monitoring process

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.290
could capture the
exit code and then

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:14.630
restart the server instantly.

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:17.650
With the advent of systems like
Kubernetes, this kind of system

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.370
was just built into the
infrastructure itself.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:22.940
Kubernetes uses an
invent loop to make sure

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.340
that resources are
healthy from containers

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:26.990
to the nodes themselves.

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:30.110
This means you no longer need
to manually run these monitoring

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.580
processes.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.460
If a resource has
failed a health check,

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.210
Kubernetes will just
automatically spin up

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.170
a replacement.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.260
But Kubernetes does a lot more
than monitor your application

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:41.505
for crashes.

00:01:41.505 --> 00:01:43.380
It can create more copies
of your application

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:44.760
to run on multiple machines.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.692
It can update your application.

00:01:46.692 --> 00:01:48.900
It can even run multiple
versions of your application

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.010
at the same time.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.190
So this means that there
are many reasons why

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:57.620
Kubernetes might terminate a
perfectly healthy container.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.400
Like if you update
your deployment

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.410
with a rolling
update, Kubernetes

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:05.580
will slowly terminate old pods,
while spinning up new ones.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:08.690
If you drain a node, Kubernetes
will terminate all pods

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:09.900
in that node.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.150
And if a node runs
out of resources,

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:15.490
Kubernetes will terminate
pods to free those resources.

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:17.490
So it's important
that your application

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:20.280
can handle termination
gracefully so

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.830
that if there is a minimal
impact on the end user

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:26.406
and there's a minimal impact
on the time to recovery.

00:02:26.406 --> 00:02:28.530
So this means that it should
save all the data that

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.470
needs to be saved, close
down any network connections,

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:33.090
finish any work that's left--

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.190
other similar tasks.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.770
So in practice, this means
your application needs

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:40.320
to handle the sigterm message.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:43.830
When it receives this message,
it should begin to shut down.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.230
Once Kubernetes decided
to terminate your pod,

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.570
a series of events takes place.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.150
Let's look at each one of
these steps in the Kubernetes

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:54.060
termination lifecycle.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:56.730
So let's say we want to
terminate one of these pods.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:00.600
At this point, the pod will
stop getting new traffic.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:03.450
Containers running in the
pod will not be affected,

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.670
but all new traffic will be
prevented from coming in.

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.280
So now let's look
at the prestop hook.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.490
The prestop hook is a
special command or HTTP

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:15.007
request that is sent to
the containers in the pod.

00:03:15.007 --> 00:03:16.590
If your application
doesn't gracefully

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.660
shut down when
receiving a sigterm,

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:22.180
you can use this hook to
trigger a graceful shutdown.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.780
So most programs gracefully shut
down when receiving a sigterm.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:28.574
But if you're using a
third-party code or some system

00:03:28.574 --> 00:03:30.240
that you don't really
have control over,

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.540
the prestop hook is a great way
to trigger a graceful shutdown,

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.576
without modifying
the application.

00:03:36.576 --> 00:03:37.950
So now, at this
point, Kubernetes

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.009
will send the sigterm signal
to the containers in the pod.

00:03:41.009 --> 00:03:43.050
This signal lets the
containers know that they're

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:44.940
going to be shut down soon.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:46.950
So your code should
listen for this event

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:49.507
and then start shutting
down cleanly at this point.

00:03:49.507 --> 00:03:51.840
So this may include stopping
any long-lived connections,

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.680
like a database connection
or a WebSocket stream,

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.940
saving the current state,
and doing things like that.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:00.240
So even if you're
using the prestop hook,

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.800
it's important
that you test what

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:05.730
happens to your application when
you send it the sigterm signal

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:08.310
so you're not surprised when
it happens in production.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:10.020
At this point,
Kubernetes will wait

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:13.710
for a specified time called
the termination grace period.

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:15.930
By default, this is 30 seconds.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:17.670
It's important to
note that this happens

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:21.970
in parallel to the prestop
hook and the sigterm signal.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:25.290
Kubernetes will not wait for
the prestop hook or the sigterm

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:27.600
signal to finish.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.390
If your app finishes shutting
down before the termination

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:33.180
grace period is done, Kubernetes
will just move to the next step

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.560
immediately.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.720
Now if your pod takes longer
than 30 seconds to shut down,

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:39.919
make sure you increase
the grace period.

00:04:39.919 --> 00:04:42.210
You can do that by setting
the termination grace period

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.340
seconds option in the pod YAML.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:48.810
For example, this example
has it set at 60 seconds.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:50.790
And now, the last step--

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:54.180
if the containers are still
running after the grace period,

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:58.140
they are sent the sigkill signal
and then forcibly removed.

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:03.040
At this point, Kubernetes cleans
up all the objects as well.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:05.760
Kubernetes terminates
pods for many reasons

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:07.790
and making sure that
your application handles

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:10.260
these terminations
gracefully is core

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:12.890
to creating a stable surface.

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:16.320
I'll see you on the next episode
of Kubernetes Best Practices.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.470
[MUSIC PLAYING]

